      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:02 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2003-02-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0d215f8bbadab977a241f078ce1a404683c90c035c225c17ca138bfae606be17",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0d215f8bbada-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The officers in this case went to respondent's apartment on a weekday afternoon to execute a valid search warrant for drugs. They knocked loudly and announced their purpose. They waited at least 15 to 20 seconds without hearing any response and then forcibly entered the apartment. Those actions were reasonable under the Fourth Amendment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2003-02-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0d215f8bbadab977a241f078ce1a404683c90c035c225c17ca138bfae606be17",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0d215f8bbada-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Did the court here say that these were the only -- the only four factors?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2003-02-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0d215f8bbadab977a241f078ce1a404683c90c035c225c17ca138bfae606be17",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0d215f8bbada-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Souter's question earlier pointed out, in a case where\nthere are no concerns that would -- that would warrant a\nmore prompt entry, we think the question is whether a\nreasonable officer could conclude that he had been\nconstructively denied admittance."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2003-02-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0d215f8bbadab977a241f078ce1a404683c90c035c225c17ca138bfae606be17",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0d215f8bbada-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The point I was attempting to make, Your Honor was that what this Court has recognized repeatedly, and it said in Ramirez it was now decisively holding and resolving the question, and that is that the statute here, 3109, is not to be read literally but merely is to be read as a codification of the common law tradition of knock and announce."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2003-02-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0d215f8bbadab977a241f078ce1a404683c90c035c225c17ca138bfae606be17",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0d215f8bbada-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This case is about whether your door, my door,\nand everyone's door is worth 15 seconds."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2003-02-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0d215f8bbadab977a241f078ce1a404683c90c035c225c17ca138bfae606be17",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0d215f8bbada-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Well, why don't we take the Ninth Circuit's own subsequent case where it has a footnote?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2003-02-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0d215f8bbadab977a241f078ce1a404683c90c035c225c17ca138bfae606be17",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0d215f8bbada-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: And what did the court below treat it as?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Roske",
      "case_id": "2003-02-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0d215f8bbadab977a241f078ce1a404683c90c035c225c17ca138bfae606be17",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0d215f8bbada-0002-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "and it has a deterrent effect. Your opinions do matter greatly."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "David B. Salmons",
      "case_id": "2003-02-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0d215f8bbadab977a241f078ce1a404683c90c035c225c17ca138bfae606be17",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0d215f8bbada-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SALMONS: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice. Unless the Court has any further questions, the Government submits that the judgment below should be reversed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "David B. Salmons",
      "case_id": "2003-02-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0d215f8bbadab977a241f078ce1a404683c90c035c225c17ca138bfae606be17",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0d215f8bbada-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SALMONS: Well, Your Honor, what I do know is that as this Court indicated in its decision in Wilson where it incorporated the common law tradition into the Fourth Amendment standard of reasonableness, that the standard at common law was a flexible one. It didn't require knock and announce in all cases, and it took into account a variety of law enforcement concerns and, we would submit, including the risk that there might be dangers to the officer's safety or that evidence might be disposed of."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court Clerk",
